KZ2 patch 6094 (fps-why 2026-11-24 R1, R2, R3, R6 first half): ppu-main's runtime overhead in the
lifted PPU code. Ported from KZ3 patch 2236 (Project Helghast, same author, MIT; KZ3 has agreed
to share one patch line for the TLS fold, the accessors and -mfma) and the PPU half of KZ3 patch 1115 (the TEB slot
for g_trampoline_fn, an earlier change, Project Helghast; its profiler half is not ported). Adapted: KZ2_ names, no per-thread icall cache
(KZ2 has none), no raw-store reservation test (KZ2's VM_RAW_ST has no SPU coherence, so there is nothing to inline),
an exact fmaf reference for the boot self-test (port/kz2/ppu_isa_ref.cpp).

Cause (fps-why 2026-11-24 section 1 row 3, the RE notes "An earlier change"): ppu-main is busy 28-36 ms per heavy frame,
12-15 ms of it ps3recomp runtime overhead: emulated TLS 13 % (__emutls_get_address -> _MCF_thread_self ->
__MCF_tls_table_get -> do_linear_probe_nonempty; TU 010 has 843 emutls relocations), out-of-line accessors 12.8 %
(about 60 .isra copies of each accessor; 8,140 of TU 010's 10,446 32-bit reads are calls), vmaddfp plus software
fmaf 9.8 %.

Change:
- runtime/ppu/ppu_vmx.h: under __FMA__ && __SSE4_1__ (x86), vmaddfp = _mm_fmadd_ps and vnmsubfp = fmsub then a sign
  flip (an exact zero must be -0), with pshufb byte swap, the NJ input/result flush and the NaN -> 0x7FC00000 fix on
  all four lanes. The scalar #else path is unchanged. NaN: a NaN operand loses its payload in both paths, as on
  main (unlike the other vector float ops here); RPCS3 (read, not copied) does the same for vmaddfp/vnmsubfp (the
  canonical 0x7FC00000 with its NaN fix on). A propagation rule waits for a hardware-backed answer (lane/runtime-6097).
  R2 IS A CORRECTNESS FIX AGAINST THE PS3, not a no-op: MinGW's software fmaf and fma are not correctly rounded
  (kz2.exe 0x149081ee0 / 0x149081f90 sum a split product with several roundings; KZ3 measured ~15 % of random inputs
  wrong). Hardware FMA rounds once, as the PS3's vmaddfp / fmadd do. So the -mfma path is checked against an exact
  reference -- glibc's fmaf in the host test, kz2_fmaf_exact (port/kz2/kz2_fmaf_exact.h) in the [ppu-isa] boot
  self-test -- and NOT against today's exe, whose bits are the wrong ones.
  KZ2_PPU_ISA ALSO CHANGES THE SCALAR FPU: -mfma on every lifted TU turns the fused family (fmadd, fmsub, fnmadd,
  fnmsub and their single forms, which go through fma() in the lifter's ppu_fmadd_core) into hardware vfmadd*. Their
  bits change wherever MinGW's software fma() was wrong: the same correctness fix against the PS3, for the scalar
  unit. (The single forms still compute fma in double and then round to float, a rare double rounding against PPU
  fmadds that predates 6094 and is not fixed here.) An ISA A/B therefore changes guest FPU results, not only speed.
- runtime/ppu/ppu_loader.cpp: t_thr (depth, vcall stack and pointer) taken once per indirect call;
  ps3_indirect_call / ps3_indirect_call_to both go through ps3_indirect_call_entry(ctx, addr) ->
  ps3_indirect_call_impl_at(ctx, addr) (no TLS flag pair; between the old set and consume only diagnostics ran,
  they still name ctx->ctr); the drain loop takes &g_trampoline_fn once. ps3_ppu_dispatch_depth_slot /
  ps3_ppu_vcall_sp_slot return the t_thr fields. ppu_tramp_ref_slow + g_ppu_tramp_slot (KZ3 1115; KZ2_TRAMP_TEB=0
  turns the slot off at run time). KZ2_PPU_COST_LOG=1 (default off): [ppu-tls] at 10 s and exit, [ppu-acc]
  slow_ld/slow_st every 10 s.
- tools/ppu_lifter.py (HEADER_PREAMBLE and SOURCE_PREAMBLE; needs a relift): ppu_mm_take, vm_mm_in/ok/st_ok,
  vm_{read,write}{8,16,32,64}_mm and vm_raw_ld16/st16 are PPU_ACC_INL: always_inline with -DPPU_ACC_ALWAYS_INLINE=1
  (KZ2_PPU_ACC_INLINE=ON), plain static inline (as before) without. With the option ON every fallback is a static
  noinline cold stub (ppu_ld*_slow, ppu_st*_slow) that calls the runtime's vm_read* / vm_write* and bumps the shared
  relaxed counter only while ppu_acc_count_on is 1 (set by KZ2_PPU_COST_LOG=1; otherwise the stub only reads that
  never-written flag, so no thread writes a shared cache line per slow access). With the option OFF the fallbacks are
  macros for the plain vm_read* / vm_write* calls: the preprocessed accessors are main's (r94 review item 1).
  Watched-path semantics unchanged. PPU_TRAMP_TEB (default 0; -DPPU_TRAMP_TEB=1 from KZ2_PPU_TRAMP_TEB=ON, MinGW x86-64 only):
  g_trampoline_fn through the TEB slot.
- port/kz2 (not in this patch): KZ2_PPU_ISA, KZ2_PPU_ACC_INLINE, KZ2_PPU_CMODEL_SMALL, KZ2_PPU_TRAMP_TEB build
  options, all default OFF (KZ3 2236 has the accessors always inlined unconditionally; here an option, for its own A/B
  and because of the compile time);
  ppu_isa_probe.cpp / ppu_isa_ref.cpp and the [ppu-isa] boot line; kz2_cpu_check.c also for KZ2_PPU_NEEDS_FMA.

Expected effect (ASSUMPTION, fps-why): TLS 3-4 ms, FMA 1.5-2.5 ms, inlining 1-2 ms of ppu-main per heavy frame; judged
on ppu-main busy ms per flip in a paired A/B, not fps. NOT verified: any game run; the gain; MinGW's software fmaf
error rate on KZ2's own inputs.

Host tests: tools/runtime/test_ppu_vmx_fma.sh, tools/runtime/test_ppu_icall_to.sh, tools/runtime/test_ppu_acc_fast.sh,
tools/runtime/test_ppu_tramp_slot.sh (each with negative controls).

